WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.370
So I remember this scene.

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:07.273
I remember this
being one of our first scenes together.

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:08.041
- Yeah. 
- Yeah.

00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:09.476
This was our first day together.

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:11.177
And it was really,

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:14.280
you know, one that would set the tone
of the entire relationship.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.418
But to be honest my memory is about

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:21.421
how much Lily affected this entire scene.

00:00:21.454 --> 00:00:23.623
We need to be quiet for a while.

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:30.797
I remember now
looking at the Redcorn family's

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:34.434
antique Spode dishes,
which they finally got back that

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:37.537
year at I'n-Lon-Schka they were feeding
people off of rented plates

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:38.204
but these have been

00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:42.108
the plates this family has used
for the last 100 years to feed people.

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:46.046
I remember asking when I had a list
of what would Molly be serving

00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:48.314
Ernest and chose the grape dumplings.

00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:50.950
I was just about
to say the grape dumplings.

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:55.121
Don't you want a bite of this?

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:56.689
They got too much sugar.

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:58.758
I decided that I'm going to serve him

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:01.161
what has the most sugar in it

00:01:01.161 --> 00:01:03.363
as a diabetic

00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:05.498
because, you know, it's

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:09.035
just kind of knowing that
that's probably what he was there for.

00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:11.337
Can never be too sweet

00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:12.806
now can you?

00:01:14.474 --> 00:01:16.242
It makes me sickly.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:19.245
By identifying him as somebody that

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.281
you know is after something

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:24.284
but she's also sitting in the same room
with him,

00:01:24.284 --> 00:01:27.554
flirting with him, courting him,
getting to know him.

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:31.858
And it's really the culmination
of what to me has been

00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:36.096
one of the most bizarre and twisted
love stories I've ever been a part of.

00:01:37.497 --> 00:01:39.132
And also this,

00:01:39.132 --> 00:01:42.535
the element of... that you were talking about
with the with the storm as well,

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:44.537
that was your input.

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:47.073
The storm. It's...

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:52.278
well it's... powerful.

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:54.781
So we need to be quiet for a while. 

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:57.050
When the storm would blow through like that

00:01:57.117 --> 00:01:59.085
it's powerful.

00:01:59.085 --> 00:02:01.955
So you need to sit
kind of in reverence of it.

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:04.157
You need to sit and receive
what it's bringing.

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:06.092
To remain quiet through all of that.

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:09.496
And my... my character's sort of...

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:11.097
Still reaching for the whiskey.

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:13.099
- Still reaching for the drinks.
- Still wanting a drink.

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:16.636
Still wanting to relate,
still wanting to have a small talk.

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:17.670
Yeah.

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:21.074
And I remember that being, you know,
you're the one who came up with this idea

00:02:21.074 --> 00:02:22.742
for the line about it.

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:24.644
It's good for the crops, that's for sure.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:26.146
Well it'll be good for the crops.

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:30.450
So it's, you know, kind of doing that
Blackbird Talk thing, filling

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.820
the silence and me just creating it
and allowing it to sit.

00:02:33.887 --> 00:02:38.158
And I remember talking to Marty, too,
about just sitting, because oftentimes

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:41.027
when you do these scenes, you're like,
“What do I do next?”

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:43.062
- Exactly! How do I fill the space?
- He had to

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:46.032
articulate quite a few times to us, “No, no, no.”

00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:48.501
“Just sit and watch the storm.”

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:50.136
It's good for the crops, that's for sure.

00:02:50.136 --> 00:02:51.538
Just be still.

